{{also|pénétrable}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|penetrable}}, {{m|enm|penytrable}}, from {{der|en|fro|penetrable}}, from {{der|en|ML.|penetrābilis}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpɛnətɹəbəl/}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Capable of being [[penetrate]]d, [[enter]]ed, or [[pierce]]d. Also figuratively.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1867|author=w:George Rawlinson|title=The Five Great Monarchies of the Ancient Eastern World
|passage=On the east the high mountain-chain of Zagros, '''penetrable''' only in one or two places, forms a barrier of the most marked character, and is beyond a doubt the natural limit for which we are looking.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1900|author=Arthur M. Mann|title=The Boer in Peace and War
|passage=A Boer may know you, but it will take you some time to know him, and when a certain stage in your acquaintance is reached, you may begin to wonder whether his real nature is '''penetrable''' at all.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1996|author=Peter Carruthers; Peter K. Smith|title=Theories of Theories of Mind
|passage=A capacity is cognitively '''penetrable''' in this sense if that capacity is affected by the subject's knowledge or ignorance of the domain.}}

